title:   Hello, TOS
level:   first steps
needs:   assembler, emulator, rom
learn:   what a GEMDOS trap is; that breakpoints are file:line; where a call leaves its answer
source:  template "GEMDOS hello" as hello.s

step  Assemble the file
  teach  {key:build} assembles hello.s beside it and maps anything the assembler
         objects to straight onto the line it objects to. Nothing here is
         screen-scraped: the file is assembled from disk, which is the only
         form this IDE knows — a document is a file, never a scratch buffer.
  do     build
  expect problems == none

step  Run it
  teach  {key:run} starts the emulator with the program and stops at its entry
         point before the first instruction has run, so you are looking at the
         machine with your program loaded and nothing done yet.
  do     run
  expect stopped

step  Stop where the program asks for a key
  teach  Breakpoints are file:line, never addresses — GEMDOS relocates the
         program on every run, so the line is the only stable name for a place
         in it. Line 16 is the Crawcin call, the same one the template's own
         header points at. {key:toggle-breakpoint} in the gutter sets one;
         Continue runs on until it fires.
  do     breakpoint 16
  do     continue
  expect stopped
  expect at hello.s:16

step  The keyboard is the emulator's
  teach  Click the emulator panel to hand it the keyboard and type any key; F12
         gives the keyboard back. Crawcin returns what you typed in D0, and the
         Registers panel is showing it — D0 in the first column, the data
         registers. A lesson cannot press the key for you, and any key is a
         correct answer.
  nominal only you can press a key in the emulator
  do     explain Crawcin
